// engine/effects/raster_dsl.emp — the Phase-3 sparse-tier raster authoring vocabulary.
//
// The general constructor covers the SPARSE tier only. A [u16; N] array cannot hold a
// link-time symbol, so a program carrying the gradient's stream pointer is not
// expressible as a word array at any spelling: the DENSE tier keeps
// raster_gradient_program in engine.effects.raster. A program mixing sparse events
// with a dense run is deliberately NOT authorable in Phase 3 (design spec 4.1) even
// though the wire format permits it — a section takes one tier or the other.
//
// This module owns the encoding arithmetic and its validation; engine.effects.raster
// owns only the decoding. Pure comptime — emits no ROM bytes; glob-imported into every
// module (the native comptime-helper set).
//
// THE LITERALS IN THE FUNCTION BODIES BELOW ARE DELIBERATE. A comptime fn's free names
// resolve at its CALL SITE, so a body spelling OP_CRAM would force every author's
// module to import engine.effects.raster's constants or get `unknown name` — and in
// struct-literal position a missing import degrades SILENTLY to a label reference. The
// module-level ensures immediately below keep the single source of truth without
// imposing that on callers. Those pins ARE the codebase's reference example of the
// pattern now: the sparse-tier helper it was originally taken from was deleted along
// with the rest of the authoring block this module replaced, so the rule outlived its
// exemplar. Follow the pins below.
//
// vdp_comm / VdpTarget / VdpOp ARE safe to name in a body: engine.vdp is itself a
// COMPTIME_HELPERS member, so it is glob-injected at every call site.
module engine.effects.raster_dsl

use engine.effects.raster.{OP_SET_REG, OP_CRAM, OP_PAL_REGION, OP_RUN_GRADIENT,
                          RASTER_OPS_END, RASTER_ARM_PARK, RASTER_CRAM_MAX,
                          RASTER_BUF_SIZE, RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE, RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE,
                          pal_stage_off}

// ---- the inlined-literal pins (see the module note above) -------------------
ensure(OP_SET_REG == 0 && OP_CRAM == 2 && OP_PAL_REGION == 4 && OP_RUN_GRADIENT == 6,
       "raster_dsl's inlined opcodes drifted from engine.effects.raster")
ensure(RASTER_OPS_END == $FFFF, "raster_dsl's inlined terminator drifted from RASTER_OPS_END")
ensure(RASTER_ARM_PARK == $8AFF, "raster_dsl's inlined park word drifted from RASTER_ARM_PARK")
ensure(RASTER_CRAM_MAX == 3, "raster_dsl's inlined per-fire colour ceiling drifted from RASTER_CRAM_MAX")
ensure(RASTER_BUF_SIZE == 128, "raster_dsl's inlined buffer bound drifted from RASTER_BUF_SIZE")
// NOTE the message spells both constant names out. `RASTER_{MIN,MAX}_FIRE_LINE` would
// be parsed as an INTERPOLATION and emit `unknown name MIN` at span 0..3 instead of the
// diagnostic — latent while the pin passes, broken exactly when it fires.
ensure(RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE == 3 && RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE == 223,
       "raster_dsl's inlined screen-line bounds drifted from RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE / RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE")
// WHY arm_at CARRIES NO PER-CALL RANGE CHECK. Reg $0A holds one 8-bit reload, so an arm
// word can express a gap of at most 255 lines. That is not a constraint an author can
// reach: the widest gap constructible is from priming fire line 1 to the last legal fire
// line, and the bound below is what makes every per-call check dead code. Assert it ONCE,
// here, where it fires if someone raises the screen-line ceiling — the only way it breaks.
ensure((RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE - 1) - 1 <= 255,
       "raster screen-line ceiling raised past what one reg $0A reload can span (255): arm_at drops its per-call gap check on the strength of this bound, so restore one there before raising it")
// NOTE this reasoning does NOT transfer to raster.emp's raster_arm, which keeps both of
// its guards. It is `pub` and takes unconstrained fire lines, so its checks are dead only
// for today's callers rather than structurally — the distinction that decides whether
// removing a guard is a cleanup or a silent loss of coverage.
// pal_region inlines the staging arithmetic rather than calling pal_stage_off (a
// call-site name); this pins the formula against its authority.
ensure(pal_stage_off(1, 3, 5) == 1 * 128 + 3 * 32 + 5 * 2,
       "raster_dsl's inlined Pal_Variant_Stage arithmetic drifted from pal_stage_off")

// ===========================================================================
// DESCRIPTORS
//
// The enum is plumbing; the AUTHORING surface is the constructors below it, which
// are where every bound is checked. The payload slots are spelled truthfully —
// `array` where the payload is an array — but that spelling is DOCUMENTATION, not a
// check: measured at Task 7, `RasterOp.Cram(addr, 5)` binds an int to the `array`
// slot without complaint, and the build only fails downstream, at the first `.len`
// or `++` (`` `len` is not a field or `.len` of int ``). The constructors' ensures
// are what actually enforce the shapes. Same class as the parameter annotations.
// ===========================================================================

// Vsram is its OWN variant rather than a target field on Cram, deliberately. The wire
// body is byte-identical in SHAPE (same OP_CRAM opcode — the handler never inspects the
// command longword, see `vsram` below), but every DERIVED fact differs: op_mask is 0 for
// VSRAM and `1 << (addr >> 5)` for CRAM, the address ceiling is 78 not 126, the "not line
// 0" rule is meaningless off CRAM, and the run-past-the-end arithmetic counts bytes of an
// 80-byte VSRAM instead of entries of a 16-colour line. A `Cram(addr, target, cols)` would
// have made every one of those an `if` inside a shared arm — the shape that lets a future
// target inherit a bound that was only ever true for CRAM. Separate variants make the
// match arms exhaustive-checked instead, which is what caught the op_mask trap.
pub comptime enum RasterOp {
    SetReg(int, int),                     // (mid-frame $8xxx word, frame-top reset word)
    Cram(int, array),                     // (CRAM byte address, colour-word ARRAY)
    PalRegion(int, int, int, int, int),   // (CRAM byte address, slot, pal line, entry, count)
    Vsram(int, array),                    // (VSRAM byte address, scroll-word ARRAY)
}

pub comptime enum RasterFire {
    Fire(int, array),                     // (screen line the effect lands on, RasterOp ARRAY)
}

// ---- op constructors --------------------------------------------------------

// set_reg — a mid-frame VDP register write, paired with the frame-top word that
// restores the SAME register. Pairing them is what makes "a mode change cannot latch
// past the frame" structural instead of a comment: raster_program derives the program's
// init words from these resets, so an author cannot write the mid-frame half alone.
pub comptime fn set_reg(word: int, reset: int) -> RasterOp {
    ensure(word >= $8000 && word <= $97FF,
           "set_reg: {word} is not a VDP register write word ($8000..$97FF)")
    ensure(reset >= $8000 && reset <= $97FF,
           "set_reg: frame-top reset {reset} is not a VDP register write word ($8000..$97FF)")
    ensure((word >> 8) == (reset >> 8),
           "set_reg: mid-frame word {word} writes VDP reg {(word >> 8) - $80} but its frame-top reset {reset} writes reg {(reset >> 8) - $80} — they must be the same register")
    // Reg $0A is the SCHEDULE's, not the author's. Raster_HInt writes the record's arm
    // word to reg $0A FIRST and only then runs the record's ops (engine/effects/raster.emp,
    // Raster_HInt: `move.w (a1)+, (a2)` before `.op_loop`), so an op that writes $8Axx
    // overwrites the arm the encoder just scheduled. The counter then reloads with the
    // author's value at the next HInt and every remaining fire in the frame lands on the
    // wrong line — silently, and only from that fire onward, which is the worst shape a
    // schedule bug can take. The word-pair check above makes this one ensure cover the
    // frame-top reset too.
    //
    // ONLY reg $0A is banned. Reg $0F (autoincrement) was considered and deliberately
    // NOT banned: Gunstar Heroes and Alien Soldier both change autoincrement mid-frame as
    // ordinary technique, and set_reg's mandatory frame-top reset already bounds the
    // change to the frame that made it.
    // NOT ROUTED THROUGH VDP_Shadow_Table — both halves go straight to VDP_CTRL (the
    // mid-frame word from Raster_HInt's .op_set_reg, the reset from Raster_VBlank's init
    // loop). Harmless today and NOT a latent bug by luck: Flush_VDP_Shadow runs right
    // after Raster_VBlank in the same VBlank but writes only registers whose dirty bit is
    // set, and nothing in the tree ever marks reg $0C dirty. It becomes real the moment
    // someone set_regs a register that IS shadowed and dirtied: the flush would override
    // this op's frame-top reset for exactly those frames, and the shadow's idea of the
    // register would permanently diverge from the hardware's. If you add one, write the
    // reset through the shadow instead of here.
    ensure((word >> 8) != $8A,
           "set_reg: {word} writes VDP reg $0A, the HInt line counter the raster SCHEDULE owns — Raster_HInt writes the record's arm word before running the ops, so this op would overwrite it and desynchronise every later fire in the frame. Change the SCHEDULE instead: the fires' screen lines are the input, and the encoder derives every arm word from them.")
    return RasterOp.SetReg(word, reset)
}

// sh_on — Shadow/Highlight ON below the fire line, H40 base restored at frame top.
// $8C89 is $8C81 | bit 3: the resolution bits are untouched, per the V28/V30 quirk
// ruling in the raster survey. $8C81 is boot's H40 base (engine/system/boot_data.emp:140).
pub comptime fn sh_on() -> RasterOp {
    return set_reg($8C89, $8C81)
}

// cram — an inline CRAM write: `colours` words starting at CRAM byte address `addr`.
pub comptime fn cram(addr: int, colours: array) -> RasterOp {
    ensure(addr >= 0 && addr <= 126, "cram: CRAM byte address {addr} outside 0..126")
    ensure((addr & 1) == 0, "cram: CRAM byte address {addr} is odd — colours are words")
    ensure((addr >> 5) != 0,
           "cram: address {addr} is on CRAM line 0, the character's line (CharacterDef.cd_palette) — a raster write there repaints the active character")
    ensure(colours.len >= 1 && colours.len <= 3,
           "cram: {colours.len} colours exceeds RASTER_CRAM_MAX (3) — that is the ~60-cycle per-fire budget, not a FIFO limit")
    ensure(((addr >> 1) & 15) + colours.len <= 16,
           "cram: {colours.len} colours from entry {(addr >> 1) & 15} runs past the end of CRAM line {addr >> 5}")
    return RasterOp.Cram(addr, colours)
}

// pal_region — a SCOPED colour swap streamed from a variant's Pal_Variant_Stage bytes.
// The destination CRAM address and the staging source must name the same line and
// entry; hand authoring had no such check.
pub comptime fn pal_region(addr: int, slot: int, pal_line: int, entry: int, count: int) -> RasterOp {
    ensure(addr >= 0 && addr <= 126, "pal_region: CRAM byte address {addr} outside 0..126")
    ensure((addr & 1) == 0, "pal_region: CRAM byte address {addr} is odd — colours are words")
    ensure(slot >= 0 && slot < 2, "pal_region: slot {slot} outside 0..PAL_MAX_VARIANTS(2)")
    ensure(pal_line >= 1 && pal_line <= 3,
           "pal_region: staging line {pal_line} outside 1..3 (line 0 is the character's)")
    ensure(entry >= 0 && entry <= 15, "pal_region: entry {entry} outside 0..15")
    ensure(count >= 1 && count <= 3,
           "pal_region: {count} colours exceeds RASTER_CRAM_MAX (3)")
    ensure(entry + count <= 16,
           "pal_region: {count} colours from entry {entry} runs past the end of line {pal_line}")
    ensure((addr >> 5) == pal_line,
           "pal_region: destination CRAM address {addr} is on line {addr >> 5} but the staging source is line {pal_line}")
    ensure(((addr >> 1) & 15) == entry,
           "pal_region: destination CRAM address {addr} is entry {(addr >> 1) & 15} but the staging source is entry {entry}")
    return RasterOp.PalRegion(addr, slot, pal_line, entry, count)
}

// vsram — a mid-frame VERTICAL SCROLL write: `values` words starting at VSRAM byte
// address `addr`. Per-band vertical scroll, i.e. the effect the whole Genesis corpus
// actually reaches for HInt to do (Batman & Robin's handlers write VSRAM and touch CRAM
// zero times; Thunder Force IV writes VSRAM only). Aeon has one vertical scroll factor
// for the whole BG plane and no vertical banding at all without this.
//
// IT NEEDED NO RUNTIME CHANGE, and that is a fact about the decoder, not luck:
// Raster_HInt's `.op_cram` does `move.l (a1)+, (a2)` with whatever command longword the
// program carries and then streams words to VDP_DATA — it never inspects the target bits.
// The ONLY thing that made those ops CRAM ops was `cram`/`pal_region` hardcoding
// VdpTarget.Cram in this encoder. So this constructor emits OP_CRAM (the literal 2, per
// the module note on inlined literals) with a VSRAM command instead.
//
// VSRAM is 80 bytes = 40 word entries: in per-column vertical scroll mode entry 2n is
// plane A and entry 2n+1 is plane B for the n-th 16-pixel column; in full-screen mode
// only entries 0 and 1 are read. Addresses are BYTE addresses into that, like `cram`'s.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURED ON ORACLE 2026-08-14: A VSRAM WRITE FROM HINT LANDS ON N+1, LIKE CRAM.
// OJZ_TestVsram authored at screen line 112 → first differing pixel row is exactly 112,
// rows 108-111 pixel-identical — measured for entry 0 (plane A) and entry 1 (plane B).
// `fire` owns the single -1 and this op inherits it unchanged; there is no
// VSRAM-specific line rule. Caveat: oracle is Exodus-derived (consults VSRAM
// continuously); GensKMod latches at HBlank start, and this project has no real
// hardware, so N+1 is the standard of evidence available, not a silicon fact. If a
// latching implementation ever measures N+2, THE FIX IS IN THIS CONSTRUCTOR'S LINE
// ARITHMETIC (return a fire scheduled one line earlier, or carry a per-op line bias
// into fire_lines), NOT in the runtime: the handler is target-agnostic by design and
// must stay that way. Evidence: docs/benchmarks/effects-p3/GATE-EVIDENCE.md.
//
// AUTHORING CAUTIONS, both learned on the gate fixture:
//   - Byte 0 (plane A, full-screen mode) is UNSAFE to scroll mid-frame in this engine:
//     the rows around the camera window are the streamer's working margin, and a large
//     shift displays that scratch space as stale/half-written tiles. Band plane B.
//   - Pick the offset against the art: a shift equal to the BG's vertical repeat
//     period is pixel-invisible (the OJZ trunks repeat every 64 px — a $0040 shift
//     showed literally nothing; $0043 banded cleanly).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
pub comptime fn vsram(addr: int, values: array) -> RasterOp {
    ensure(addr >= 0 && addr <= 78,
           "vsram: VSRAM byte address {addr} outside 0..78 (VSRAM is 80 bytes = 40 word entries; entry 2n is plane A of the n-th 16-pixel column, 2n+1 is plane B)")
    ensure((addr & 1) == 0, "vsram: VSRAM byte address {addr} is odd — scroll values are words")
    ensure(values.len >= 1 && values.len <= 3,
           "vsram: {values.len} words exceeds RASTER_CRAM_MAX (3). The ceiling is the per-fire CYCLE budget, not a CRAM or FIFO limit, so a VSRAM write spends it exactly like a colour write does: same dispatch, same EFX_BLANK_DELAY, same word loop.")
    ensure(addr + 2 * values.len <= 80,
           "vsram: {values.len} words from byte {addr} runs past the end of VSRAM (80 bytes)")
    return RasterOp.Vsram(addr, values)
}

// ---- fire constructors ------------------------------------------------------

// fire — one scheduled event. `line` is the SCREEN line the effect lands on; the DSL
// derives the fire line (line - 1) and the arm schedule. The floor of 3 is the priming
// records': fires 0/1 prime on lines 0-1, so the earliest real fire is 2, landing on 3.
// Lines 0-2 are the init words' job.
pub comptime fn fire(line: int, ops: array) -> RasterFire {
    ensure(line >= 3 && line <= 223,
           "fire: screen line {line} outside 3..223 (lines 0-2 belong to the priming records and the init words)")
    ensure(ops.len >= 1, "fire: a fire with no ops — drop the fire rather than authoring an empty one")
    // ---- THE THREE PER-FIRE CEILINGS, AND WHAT THEY DO NOT DO -------------------
    // These are STRUCTURAL COUNTS — the same class of guard as RASTER_CRAM_MAX itself —
    // and NOT a cycle model. A cycle model was considered and rejected: the only measured
    // figures in the tree (docs/benchmarks/effects-p2/GATE-EVIDENCE.md) are per-frame upper
    // bounds that include profiler instrumentation and exception entry, so a per-op cycle
    // table derived from them would be authoritative-looking fiction. Counts are honest.
    //
    //   1. ops per fire        <= 4   every op walks the handler's compare chain, and
    //                                 OP_SET_REG — the op with no other cost — is that
    //                                 chain's FALL-THROUGH (raster.emp, Raster_HInt
    //                                 .op_loop), so it is the most expensive to DISPATCH.
    //   2. CRAM-class ops      <= 2   each of cram/pal_region/vsram pays its own dispatch
    //                                 AND its own EFX_BLANK_DELAY spin (.op_cram /
    //                                 .op_region), and that spin is the whole mechanism
    //                                 that parks a write in HBlank.
    //   3. CRAM-class words    <= 3   RASTER_CRAM_MAX — the writes themselves.
    // All three are PER-FIRE. `cram`/`pal_region`/`vsram` also bound their OWN word count,
    // which is necessary and not sufficient: three 3-word ops satisfy every per-op guard.
    //
    // WHAT IS STILL NOT ENFORCED — read no cycle guarantee into the above. The COST of a
    // fire is not modelled at all: a 4-op fire of set_regs and a 1-op fire of one cram both
    // pass, and the first is certainly slower. Whether even the permitted maxima finish
    // before active display is UNMEASURED — nothing in this tree has ever run more than two
    // ops or more than ONE CRAM-class op in a single fire (both shipped fixtures are
    // set_reg + one CRAM-class op), and adjacent-fire density is unmeasured too. These
    // ceilings bound the DAMAGE of a mistake; they do not certify the shapes they admit.
    // The predecessor of this comment claimed RASTER_CRAM_MAX was "a PER-FIRE cycle budget
    // … on the whole fire" while the code counted only CRAM words and no ops at all; that
    // gap is exactly why a 20-set_reg fire passed every guard.
    comptime var cram_words = 0
    comptime var cram_ops = 0
    for o in ops {
        cram_words = cram_words + op_cram_words(o)
        if op_is_set_reg(o) == 0 { cram_ops = cram_ops + 1 }
    }
    ensure(ops.len <= 4,
           "fire at screen line {line}: {ops.len} ops exceeds the per-fire ceiling of 4. Every op costs a walk of Raster_HInt's compare chain before it does any work, and OP_SET_REG — the cheap-looking one — is the chain's fall-through, so it is the most expensive op to dispatch. Four is a structural ceiling, not a measured one: the richest fire anything has ever run is two ops. Split the work across consecutive fires.")
    ensure(cram_ops <= 2,
           "fire at screen line {line}: {cram_ops} CRAM-class ops (cram / pal_region / vsram) exceeds the per-fire ceiling of 2. Each one issues its own command longword and burns its own EFX_BLANK_DELAY spin, and that spin is what parks the write offscreen — only the FIRST op's writes are measured to land in HBlank (row 119, 1px -> 0px, docs/benchmarks/effects-p2/GATE-EVIDENCE.md). Author ONE CRAM-class op per fire and use consecutive fires; a second one on the same line is admitted for the case where you have measured that you can live with its dot.")
    ensure(cram_words <= 3,
           "fire at screen line {line}: {cram_words} CRAM-class words across {ops.len} ops exceeds RASTER_CRAM_MAX (3). That is a PER-FIRE budget: split the writes across consecutive fires — a full 16-colour line takes ceil(16/3) = 6 of them, one per scanline. That six-fire idiom is OURS, not a reference's: S3K's HInt3 swaps a whole water palette in ONE interrupt (park the counter, stop the Z80, then repeat: fresh CRAM command, 3 colours, a cycle-counted ~370-cycle dbf spin), and S3K's HInt2, Sonic 2's PalToCRAM and S.C.E.'s HInt each write 64 CRAM words in a single fire with no delay at all. Ours is dot-free by construction and pays six exception entries for it; the spacing in S3K comes from the spin, not from multiple interrupts.")
    return RasterFire.Fire(line, ops)
}

// region_boundary — the composite the water cluster is: at `line`, optionally turn
// Shadow/Highlight on, then swap the region. SET_REG comes first BY CONSTRUCTION here,
// so the mixed-fire invariant is structural for this constructor and only checked for
// hand-assembled fires.
//
// `sh` HAS NO DEFAULT, deliberately. sh:0 yields a program with ZERO init words, which
// moves the priming arm word off word 3 — and Raster_PatchWaterLine writes byte offset
// 6 (WATER_TEMPLATE_ARM0_OFF, raster.emp:561) unconditionally at three sites
// (:635, :644, :647). On a patched template that would overwrite the priming record's
// op_count with an arm word, and Raster_HInt would then loop tens of thousands of times
// over garbage. A `= 0` default made the corrupt case the one an author gets by simply
// not thinking about Shadow/Highlight, so the parameter is required instead. The
// init_count == 1 assertion in Task 8 is the co-located half of this guard.
pub comptime fn region_boundary(line: int, addr: int, slot: int, pal_line: int,
                                entry: int, count: int, sh: int) -> RasterFire {
    ensure(sh == 0 || sh == 1, "region_boundary: sh {sh} must be 0 or 1")
    if sh == 1 {
        return fire(line, [sh_on(), pal_region(addr, slot, pal_line, entry, count)])
    }
    return fire(line, [pal_region(addr, slot, pal_line, entry, count)])
}

// ---- composition -------------------------------------------------------------

// compose — merge N fire lists into one program's fire list. THE point of a preset
// library: presets that do not combine force a combinatorial pile of hand-merged
// variants, which is the thing a preset library exists to avoid.
//
// It walks screen lines in ascending order rather than sorting, which buys three
// properties without a sort's off-by-ones:
//   - the result is in strictly ascending line order, which fire_lines requires;
//   - two presets firing on the SAME line become ONE fire rather than a duplicate-line
//     error, which is what "layer these two effects" has to mean;
//   - every SetReg is emitted before every CRAM-class op, so ruling 14's prefix
//     invariant holds BY CONSTRUCTION for merged fires (check_mixed_fire still runs,
//     but it can no longer be tripped by the act of composing).
// Each merged op list goes back through `fire`, so the per-fire ceilings apply to the
// COMPOSITION and not merely to its parts — composing two legal presets into an illegal
// fire is a build error, not a surprise at runtime.
//
// WHAT IT CANNOT RECONCILE, and this is the current limit of composability: two presets
// that touch the SAME VDP register with DIFFERENT frame-top resets are refused by
// prog_init. That guard is correct (the later reset silently won and left the register
// wrong for the whole frame), but it means presets must AGREE about registers, i.e.
// composition is a negotiation. The corpus answer is a blanket VBlank register restore
// (Gunstar re-blits regs $01-$12 from a RAM shadow in ~290 cycles), which would delete
// the `reset` parameter entirely. Byte-changing; booked. Do not treat this limit as
// permanent when designing presets.
pub comptime fn compose(progs: array) -> array {
    ensure(progs.len >= 1, "compose: nothing to compose")
    comptime var out = []
    for line in RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE..RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE + 1 {
        comptime var ops   = []
        comptime var found = 0
        for p in progs {
            for f in p {
                if fire_screen_line(f) == line {
                    found = 1
                    for o in fire_ops(f) {
                        if op_is_set_reg(o) == 1 { ops = ops ++ [o] }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        for p in progs {
            for f in p {
                if fire_screen_line(f) == line {
                    for o in fire_ops(f) {
                        if op_is_set_reg(o) == 0 { ops = ops ++ [o] }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if found == 1 { out = out ++ [fire(line, ops)] }
    }
    return out
}

// ---- the starter preset library ----------------------------------------------
// A preset is a comptime fn returning a FIRE LIST, so presets take parameters and
// compose (see `compose`). No new type was needed for this — the language already
// allowed it and only `compose` was missing.
//
// The `fx_` prefix is a namespace, not decoration: this module is a COMPTIME_HELPERS
// member, so every public name here is glob-injected into EVERY module in the tree, and
// tools/emp_helper_closure.py is the collision gate. Run it when adding a preset.

// fx_sh_below — Shadow/Highlight ON from `line` down, H40 base restored at frame top.
// The cheapest dramatic effect the hardware has: one register write, no CRAM traffic,
// and it darkens everything below the line including sprites.
pub comptime fn fx_sh_below(line: int) -> array {
    return [ fire(line, [ sh_on() ]) ]
}

// fx_vscroll_split — plane B's vertical scroll snaps to `offset` from `line` down, so the
// background stops tracking the camera below it. Vertical scroll BANDING.
//
// PICK THE OFFSET AGAINST THE ART. A shift equal to the background's vertical repeat
// period is pixel-invisible: the OJZ trunks repeat every 64 px, and a $0040 shift showed
// literally nothing while $0043 banded cleanly. That cost a full session on 2026-08-14 —
// read the period off the nametable before choosing.
//
// PLANE B, NOT PLANE A. Mid-frame vertical scroll of plane A displays the streamer's
// working margin (TILE_CACHE_ROWS = 60) as stale/half-written tiles.
pub comptime fn fx_vscroll_split(line: int, offset: int) -> array {
    return [ fire(line, [ vsram(2, [offset]) ]) ]
}

// fx_tint_band — from `line` down, swap `count` colours of the palette variant staged in
// `slot` into CRAM, optionally turning Shadow/Highlight on at the same line. This is the
// water-boundary shape generalised, and the CRAM destination is DERIVED from
// pal_line/entry via pal_stage_off rather than asked for a second time — pal_region's
// address/line/entry agreement checks then cannot fail, because there is only one source
// of the fact. (slot 0 in the pal_stage_off call is deliberate: the CRAM destination is
// slot-independent; `slot` selects the staging SOURCE and is passed through below.)
//
// UNCHECKABLE HERE, AND REAL: the variant bound to `slot` at runtime must cover
// `pal_line` in its `lines` mask, or Palette_DeriveVariant never writes that staging line
// and this streams whatever the buffer holds — zeros, or a previous variant's colours
// after a rebind. Binding is a runtime call (Palette_SetVariant), so a comptime guard
// cannot see it. Booked; see the vocabulary review §5.
pub comptime fn fx_tint_band(line: int, slot: int, pal_line: int, entry: int,
                             count: int, sh: int) -> array {
    return [ region_boundary(line:     line,
                             addr:     pal_stage_off(0, pal_line, entry),
                             slot:     slot,
                             pal_line: pal_line,
                             entry:    entry,
                             count:    count,
                             sh:       sh) ]
}

// ===========================================================================
// ENCODING
// ===========================================================================

comptime fn fire_screen_line(f: RasterFire) -> int {
    return match f { Fire(m, ops) => m }
}
comptime fn fire_ops(f: RasterFire) {
    return match f { Fire(m, ops) => ops }
}

// op_words — the emitted body of one op.
comptime fn op_words(o: RasterOp) {
    return match o {
        SetReg(w, reset) => [0, w],
        Cram(a, cols)    => [2,
                             vdp_comm(a, VdpTarget.Cram, VdpOp.Write) >> 16,
                             vdp_comm(a, VdpTarget.Cram, VdpOp.Write) & $FFFF,
                             cols.len - 1] ++ cols,
        PalRegion(a, slot, pl, e, n) => [4,
                             vdp_comm(a, VdpTarget.Cram, VdpOp.Write) >> 16,
                             vdp_comm(a, VdpTarget.Cram, VdpOp.Write) & $FFFF,
                             n - 1,
                             slot * 128 + pl * 32 + e * 2],
        // OP_CRAM (2) with a VSRAM command longword — the handler decodes the opcode,
        // never the target bits inside the command (see `vsram`).
        Vsram(a, vals)   => [2,
                             vdp_comm(a, VdpTarget.Vsram, VdpOp.Write) >> 16,
                             vdp_comm(a, VdpTarget.Vsram, VdpOp.Write) & $FFFF,
                             vals.len - 1] ++ vals,
    }
}

// op_size — the SAME fact by an independent path from op_words. The cross-check that
// actually runs is raster_program's own `ensure(out.len == raster_words(fires))` at the
// bottom of this module: it fires on EVERY call, and it is what catches header/record
// FRAMING drift. The `data X: [u16; raster_words(P)] = raster_program(P)` annotation adds
// one narrower thing on top — it catches `[u16; raster_words(A)] = raster_program(B)`,
// an annotation naming a DIFFERENT program, which is a realistic copy-paste between two
// adjacent fixtures — and it pins the declared ROM footprint at the linker seam. Writing
// `[u16; P.len] = P` instead would be tautological and is forbidden. A wrong word VALUE
// inside a correctly-sized body is caught by neither; that is the hand-word twins' job,
// with the goldens behind them.
comptime fn op_size(o: RasterOp) -> int {
    return match o {
        SetReg(w, reset)             => 2,
        Cram(a, cols)                => 4 + cols.len,
        PalRegion(a, slot, pl, e, n) => 5,
        Vsram(a, vals)               => 4 + vals.len,
    }
}

// op_cram_words — data words this op streams to the VDP data port, which `fire` sums
// against the per-fire ceiling. Distinct from op_size (wire words): a PalRegion is 5 wire
// words but streams `n` colours, and a SetReg is 2 wire words but streams none. The name
// says CRAM for the ceiling it feeds (RASTER_CRAM_MAX); a Vsram op counts here too,
// because the ceiling is a CYCLE budget and a VSRAM word costs exactly what a colour does.
comptime fn op_cram_words(o: RasterOp) -> int {
    return match o {
        SetReg(w, reset)             => 0,
        Cram(a, cols)                => cols.len,
        PalRegion(a, slot, pl, e, n) => n,
        Vsram(a, vals)               => vals.len,
    }
}

// op_mask — the pal_dirty_mask bit an op needs re-asserted at frame top. CRAM byte
// address >> 5 is the palette line (32 bytes = 16 words per line). THIS is what makes
// a mid-frame write transient; a mask naming any other line leaves the write latched
// forever, which is the observed P1 bug (the red covered the whole ground, not just
// below the split, when the mask said %0001 and the op wrote line 2).
comptime fn op_mask(o: RasterOp) -> int {
    return match o {
        SetReg(w, reset)             => 0,
        Cram(a, cols)                => 1 << (a >> 5),
        PalRegion(a, slot, pl, e, n) => 1 << (a >> 5),
        // ZERO, and not by omission: a VSRAM op writes no palette, so there is no line to
        // re-assert. `1 << (a >> 5)` on a VSRAM address computes a palette line from a
        // scroll offset — nonsense in general, and at VSRAM offset 0 it yields bit 0, the
        // CHARACTER's CRAM line, which would force a spurious full re-assert of the
        // character palette every single frame. Silent, and per-frame.
        Vsram(a, vals)               => 0,
    }
}

// op_init — the frame-top reset word an op requires, if any.
comptime fn op_init(o: RasterOp) {
    return match o {
        SetReg(w, reset)             => [reset],
        Cram(a, cols)                => [],
        PalRegion(a, slot, pl, e, n) => [],
        Vsram(a, vals)               => [],
    }
}

// op_is_set_reg — 1 for the one op that writes a register with NO blanking delay, 0 for
// every "CRAM-class" op (cram / pal_region / vsram: command longword, EFX_BLANK_DELAY,
// word loop). That split is the runtime's, not a taxonomy: it is what makes both the
// ordering rule (check_mixed_fire) and the CRAM-class op ceiling (fire) mean something.
comptime fn op_is_set_reg(o: RasterOp) -> int {
    return match o {
        SetReg(w, reset)             => 1,
        Cram(a, cols)                => 0,
        PalRegion(a, slot, pl, e, n) => 0,
        Vsram(a, vals)               => 0,
    }
}

// prog_mask — OR of every op's mask bit.
comptime fn prog_mask(fires: array) -> int {
    comptime var m = 0
    for f in fires {
        for o in fire_ops(f) {
            m = m | op_mask(o)
        }
    }
    return m
}

// prog_init — the distinct frame-top reset words, in first-appearance order.
//
// THE DEDUPE KEY IS THE REGISTER, NOT THE WORD. Keying on the whole word looks like the
// same thing and is not: two set_regs on the SAME register with DIFFERENT resets are two
// distinct words, so both were emitted, and Raster_HInt writes init words in array order
// (`move.w (a1)+, (a2)` / `dbf`) — so the later one silently won and the earlier op's
// intended reset was discarded for the whole frame. `set_reg` already guarantees a word
// and its own reset name the same register; nothing compared two different set_regs.
//
// Example that used to build green: fire(100, [set_reg($8C89, $8C81)]) with
// fire(140, [set_reg($8C8B, $8C83)]) emitted init = [$8C81, $8C83], leaving reg $0C at
// $8C83 — interlace ON, permanently, from a program that only asked for Shadow/Highlight.
//
// It also mattered structurally: init_count is the water patch offset's input
// (WATER_TEMPLATE_ARM0_OFF is valid only at init_count == 1), so a silently duplicated
// register moved the arm word the runtime patches unconditionally.
comptime fn prog_init(fires: array) {
    comptime var out = []
    for f in fires {
        for o in fire_ops(f) {
            for w in op_init(o) {
                comptime var seen = 0
                for x in out {
                    if (x >> 8) == (w >> 8) {
                        seen = 1
                        ensure(x == w,
                               "raster program: two frame-top resets for VDP reg {(w >> 8) - $80} disagree ({x} and {w}). Every set_reg on one register must carry the SAME reset word — the init words are written in order at frame top, so the last one silently wins and the other op's restore never happens.")
                    }
                }
                if seen == 0 { out = out ++ [w] }
            }
        }
    }
    return out
}

// ---- the measured density model (docs/benchmarks/effects-p3/DENSITY-EVIDENCE.md) ----
// Two measured points, 2026-08-14 on oracle, as DIFFERENTIALS within one shape so the
// instrument's constant terms cancel:
//     1-word vsram fire   454 cyc        3-colour cram fire   526 cyc
// Those two determine a line in the number of CRAM-class words: 418 + 36*words.
// An NTSC scanline is 128000/262 = 488.5; 488 is used, floored, so the comparison is
// conservative in the direction that ADMITS rather than refuses.
//
// IT LUMPS VSRAM IN WITH CRAM, WHICH IS KNOWN TO BE PESSIMISTIC. The 2026-08-14 corpus
// sweep found that only CRAM writes glitch — a CRAM write during rasterisation recolours
// the pixel being drawn, while VRAM, VSRAM and register writes produce no mid-line
// artifact at all. If that holds here, `vsram` inheriting EFX_BLANK_DELAY and the
// RASTER_CRAM_MAX ceiling is pure loss (Ristar writes 42 VSRAM words in ONE fire).
// Splitting the op class is booked and byte-changing. It only ever makes fires CHEAPER,
// so nothing this model admits today becomes illegal under the split.
//
// WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT COUNT: OP_SET_REG. Its dispatch cost is UNMEASURED — the
// fire-ceiling comment above notes it is the compare chain's fall-through and therefore
// the most expensive op to dispatch, but no number exists. Rather than invent one, a
// set_reg contributes ZERO here, so the model UNDER-states cost and the guard fires only
// on evidence. A set_reg-only fire is unmodelled and is never refused. Say so out loud
// wherever this model is cited.
pub const RASTER_FIRE_BASE_CYC  = 418
pub const RASTER_CRAM_WORD_CYC  = 36
pub const RASTER_SCANLINE_CYC   = 488
ensure(RASTER_FIRE_BASE_CYC + RASTER_CRAM_WORD_CYC * 1 == 454,
       "density model drifted from the measured 1-word vsram fire (454 cyc)")
ensure(RASTER_FIRE_BASE_CYC + RASTER_CRAM_WORD_CYC * 3 == 526,
       "density model drifted from the measured 3-colour cram fire (526 cyc)")

// fire_cost_cycles — modelled cost of one fire. Each CRAM-class op pays its own dispatch
// AND its own EFX_BLANK_DELAY spin, so the base is per-OP, not per-fire.
comptime fn fire_cost_cycles(f: RasterFire) -> int {
    comptime var c = 0
    for o in fire_ops(f) {
        if op_is_set_reg(o) == 0 {
            c = c + RASTER_FIRE_BASE_CYC + RASTER_CRAM_WORD_CYC * op_cram_words(o)
        }
    }
    return c
}

// check_density — a fire must finish before the next one is due. The budget between two
// fires is the number of scanlines between them. Overrunning does NOT drop the next fire
// (the counter is already armed) but pushes its writes into active display, which is
// measured as a visible mid-row colour change (DENSITY-EVIDENCE rows 111-113, transitions
// at x = 232/248/294 of 320). The last fire has nothing after it and is unconstrained.
//
// THIS CANNOT LIVE IN `fire`. Density is a property of the SCHEDULE — one fire cannot see
// its neighbour — which is exactly why the per-fire ceilings above could not catch it.
comptime fn check_density(fires: array) -> int {
    comptime var i = 0
    for f in fires {
        if i + 1 < fires.len {
            let gap  = fire_screen_line(fires[i + 1]) - fire_screen_line(f)
            let cost = fire_cost_cycles(f)
            ensure(cost <= gap * RASTER_SCANLINE_CYC,
                   "raster program: the fire at screen line {fire_screen_line(f)} models at {cost} cycles but only {gap} scanline(s) = {gap * RASTER_SCANLINE_CYC} cycles remain before the fire at {fire_screen_line(fires[i + 1])}. MEASURED 2026-08-14: a 3-colour CRAM fire costs 526 cycles against a 488-cycle line, so consecutive colour fires overrun and paint a visible mid-line colour change. Either space these fires further apart, or move per-line colour work to the DENSE tier (raster_gradient_program), which stays inside ONE interrupt instead of paying an exception entry per line — that is how S3K writes 3 colours per line at 484. Note this model does not count OP_SET_REG at all, so the true cost is higher than the number above.")
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return 0
}

// fire_lines — the full fire-line list: the two priming fires on lines 0 and 1, then
// one per authored event at (screen line - 1). Ruling 1a: the event fire must be at
// M-1 so its writes land on M. This is the SPARSE rule; the dense tier's T-1 setup
// line is a different, measured fact and must not be applied here.
comptime fn fire_lines(fires: array) {
    comptime var out = [0, 1]
    comptime var prev = 1
    for f in fires {
        let fl = fire_screen_line(f) - 1
        ensure(fl > prev,
               "raster program: fires must be in strictly ascending screen-line order, and two events cannot share a fire line (fire line {fl} follows {prev})")
        out = out ++ [fl]
        prev = fl
    }
    return out
}

// arm_at — the arm word for record i. Ruling 1b: the word WRITTEN at record i is
// consumed at the next reload and schedules gap(L[i+1] -> L[i+2]); past the end it
// parks the counter 255 lines away, i.e. never within active display.
// BOTH HALVES OF THE OLD PER-CALL ensure WERE UNREACHABLE, so it is gone and the fact it
// was reaching for is asserted ONCE, at module level, against the bounds that make it true
// (see RASTER_FIRE_SPAN below). `gap <= 255` could not fire because `fire` bounds screen
// lines to 3..223, capping any gap at 220; `gap >= 0` could not fire because
// raster_program calls fire_lines FIRST, and fire_lines already ensures strict ascent.
// A guard that cannot fire is not free — this codebase has a documented history of them,
// and each one reads as protection that is not there. The module-level version DOES fire
// if someone raises the screen-line ceiling, which is the only way the fact can break.
comptime fn arm_at(L: array, i: int) -> int {
    if i + 2 >= L.len { return $8AFF }
    return $8A00 | (L[i + 2] - L[i + 1] - 1)
}

// check_mixed_fire — ruling 14. In a fire mixing OP_SET_REG with a CRAM-class op, the
// SET_REGs must form a PREFIX: every one of them before every CRAM-class op. OP_SET_REG
// writes with no delay while every CRAM-class op burns EFX_BLANK_DELAY first, so a SET_REG
// placed after one executes strictly later in the line — a worse artifact than the measured
// one, and invisible to an author.
//
// THE PREDICATE IS A PREFIX TEST, not "a SetReg is first". The original spelling
// (`first_set == 0`) passed `[sh_on(), pal_region(..), set_reg(..)]` — two SetRegs, the
// first at index 0, the second running after the CRAM op, i.e. exactly the ordering this
// guard exists to forbid — and then reported "it is at index 0", which pointed at the one
// op that was fine. Test the LAST SetReg against the FIRST CRAM-class op.
comptime fn check_mixed_fire(f: RasterFire) -> int {
    comptime var n_set = 0
    comptime var n_cram = 0
    comptime var last_set = -1
    comptime var first_cram = -1
    comptime var i = 0
    for o in fire_ops(f) {
        if op_is_set_reg(o) == 1 {
            n_set = n_set + 1
            last_set = i
        }
        if op_is_set_reg(o) == 0 {
            n_cram = n_cram + 1
            if first_cram < 0 { first_cram = i }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    ensure(n_set == 0 || n_cram == 0 || last_set < first_cram,
           "raster fire at screen line {fire_screen_line(f)}: in a mixed fire EVERY OP_SET_REG must precede EVERY CRAM-class op, but the SET_REG at index {last_set} runs after the CRAM-class op at index {first_cram}. A mixed fire already switches its mode register ~45% across the line (measured, engine/effects/raster.emp, the row-119 note); a SET_REG placed after a CRAM-class op is pushed strictly later still, because those ops burn EFX_BLANK_DELAY and SET_REG does not. Move the register writes to the front of the op list, or schedule a pixel-clean mode change a line earlier.")
    return 0
}

// ---- the two public entry points --------------------------------------------

// raster_words — the word count, computed from op_size. Use it as the length
// annotation on the `pub data`:
//     pub data P: [u16; raster_words(PROG)] = raster_program(PROG)
// NOT `[u16; PROG.len] = PROG`, which is tautological. Note the guard only bites on a
// `data` declaration — `const` does not enforce its declared array length.
pub comptime fn raster_words(fires: array) -> int {
    comptime var n = 2 + 4 + 2            // mask + init_count, two priming records, terminator
    n = n + prog_init(fires).len
    for f in fires {
        n = n + 2                          // the record's arm word + op_count
        for o in fire_ops(f) {
            n = n + op_size(o)
        }
    }
    return n
}

// raster_program — the flat program. Header, two priming records, one record per
// authored fire in ascending screen-line order, terminator.
pub comptime fn raster_program(fires: array) {
    ensure(fires.len >= 1, "raster_program: a program with no fires — use Raster_Program_None")
    let L = fire_lines(fires)
    check_density(fires)
    let init = prog_init(fires)
    comptime var out = [prog_mask(fires), init.len] ++ init
    out = out ++ [arm_at(L, 0), 0]        // fire 0 — priming, line 0
    out = out ++ [arm_at(L, 1), 0]        // fire 1 — priming, line 1
    comptime var i = 0
    for f in fires {
        check_mixed_fire(f)
        comptime var body = []
        for o in fire_ops(f) {
            body = body ++ op_words(o)
        }
        out = out ++ [arm_at(L, i + 2), fire_ops(f).len] ++ body
        i = i + 1
    }
    out = out ++ [$8AFF, $FFFF]           // terminator — reached only by a stray fire
    // Raster_VBlank and Raster_InstallWater both copy a FIXED RASTER_BUF_SIZE bytes
    // into Buf_A / Buf_B, so a program longer than the buffer would be truncated live.
    // (The converse over-read of a short template is pre-existing and harmless — the
    // walker never reaches past the terminator. Booked as EFX-4 in docs/BUGS.md; the
    // ensure below closes that entry's overflow half and the over-read half stays open.)
    ensure(out.len * 2 <= 128,
           "raster_program: {out.len} words = {out.len * 2} bytes exceeds RASTER_BUF_SIZE (128) — Raster_VBlank and Raster_InstallWater copy a fixed 128 bytes")
    ensure(out.len == raster_words(fires),
           "raster_program: emitted {out.len} words but raster_words counted {raster_words(fires)} — the two encoding paths disagree")
    return out
}

// ---- development instruments -------------------------------------------------

// first_mismatch — -1 when the two word lists are equal, else the index of the first
// differing element. The development instrument for re-expressing a hand-authored
// program through the DSL (design spec 8.1).
//
// PRECONDITION — it MUST be paired with a separate `.len` ensure, and that is not just
// "a length difference is a different failure than a value difference". It walks only
// `a`'s indices and skips any index past the end of `b`, so it returns -1 whenever `a`
// is a PREFIX of `b` — a DSL program that is short by a trailing record compares EQUAL
// to its hand-word twin. Nothing inside this function can see that; only the length
// check can. (In this codebase the chain also holds one rung deeper, because
// raster_program asserts `out.len == raster_words(fires)` on every call, but callers
// must not rely on that: it is a fact about raster_program, not about first_mismatch.)
pub comptime fn first_mismatch(a: array, b: array) -> int {
    for i in 0..a.len {
        if i < b.len {
            if a[i] != b[i] { return i }
        }
    }
    return -1
}
